# A Comparative Analysis of Feature Selection Methods

Information Imbalance (II), Differentiable Information Imbalance (DII), and
Mutual Information (MI) compared under a shared two-level fair-comparison
framework, extended to MINE, Random Forest, and InfoNCE — evaluated on a
synthetic benchmark with known ground truth and three independent real-world
datasets. Prepared as a poster for a Bernoulli Society statistics conference.

## Overview

Feature selection methods disagree with each other more often than intuition
suggests, and that disagreement is informative rather than noise: it reflects
what each method is actually measuring (correlation, k-NN geometry, or a
learned neural bound) and how each one scales with dimensionality. This
project runs six methods head-to-head under one controlled experimental
protocol:

1. **Synthetic benchmark** (p=27/50/105, known ground-truth ranking including
   a synergistic XOR-type feature pair invisible to per-feature methods) —
   where "correct" is measurable directly.
2. **Three real datasets** (FRED-MD macro-financial panel, MI-complications
   clinical data, trading time series) — where there is no ground truth, so
   methods are instead validated by whether their selected features actually
   improve downstream k-NN classification accuracy over a random baseline,
   with bootstrap-quantified uncertainty on that advantage.

## Methods compared

| Method | What it measures | Level(s) evaluated |
|---|---|---|
| **MI** (Mutual Information) | Statistical dependence, k-NN (Kraskov–Stögbauer–Grassberger) estimator | per-feature, joint |
| **II** (Information Imbalance) | Rank-based nearest-neighbour geometry: II(X→Y) = (2/N²) Σᵢ rank_Y(NN_X(i)) | per-feature, joint (leave-one-out) |
| **DII** (Differentiable Information Imbalance) | Gradient-optimized soft relaxation of II (softmax neighbourhoods), with L1 regularization on feature weights for sparsity | joint, gradient-optimized |
| **MINE** | Neural MI estimator, Donsker–Varadhan bound with bias-corrected (EMA) gradient | joint |
| **RF** (Random Forest) | Permutation importance from a general-purpose ML model | joint |
| **InfoNCE** | Contrastive MI lower bound, softmax-based, capped at log(batch size) | joint (p=27 only, not bootstrapped) |

JMI (greedy conditional-MI selection) was also run on the synthetic benchmark
as a scalability reference: its O(p²) cost makes it intractable well before
DII or the joint/gradient-based methods, which is itself part of the result.

## Datasets

| Dataset | N | p | Target | Role |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic benchmark | 2,000 | 27 / 50 / 105 | continuous Y, known generative process incl. one XOR-type synergy pair | ground-truth check |
| FRED-MD | 794 | 120 | S&P 500 monthly return, median-split binarized | independent real-data domain (macro-financial, high-dim) |
| MI complications | 1,700 | 107 | any of 11 binary post-infarction complications | independent real-data domain (clinical, high-dim, no ground truth) |
| Trading (`train2.csv`) | 5,000 (stratified subsample of 440,402) | 27 | next-day price direction, native binary (1=up, 0=down, ~50.3% positive) | real-data domain, **not** high-dimensional — complementary, not a third high-dim confirmation |

## Methodology

- **Two-level fair comparison**: every method is evaluated both per-feature
  (independent scores) and jointly (leave-one-out backward elimination, or
  direct joint gradient optimization for DII), so no method is compared
  per-feature against another's joint result.
- **Bootstrap CI**: 80% subsampling *without* replacement (not classic
  with-replacement bootstrap — chosen to avoid zero-distance duplicate-
  neighbour artifacts in the distance-based methods, II and DII), ranking
  recomputed from scratch on every draw.
- **Ground-truth-free downstream validation** (the 3 real datasets): k-NN
  leave-one-out balanced accuracy using each method's top-K features,
  compared against K random features (n=100 random draws), reported as an
  accuracy advantage with a bootstrap 95% CI. An advantage is called
  *robust* only if its CI excludes zero.
- **Multiple-testing correction**: MI complications tests 11 complications ×
  4 methods × 4 K values (176 tests); Benjamini–Hochberg correction leaves 7
  survivors, of which only 3 are *also* bootstrap-robust — BH significance
  and CI-excludes-zero robustness do not always agree, which is reported
  honestly rather than picking whichever criterion looks stronger.

## Key findings

**DII+L1 is the only method with a robust downstream advantage in all four
datasets** — the closest thing to a consistent winner across domains:

- **Synthetic**: DII+L1 has the highest bootstrap-mean τ at p=27 (0.370
  [0.302, 0.446]) and stays within rank 1–3 of the known XOR synergy pair at
  every p. MI_joint degenerates (Kraskov estimator collapses to 0) at
  p≥50; JMI becomes computationally intractable above p=27; II_joint's XOR
  detection collapses to rank ~22–70 at p=105.
- **FRED-MD**: DII+L1 robust at all 4 K (advantage +0.075 to +0.097); MI
  per-feature robust only at K=5,10; II (per-feature and joint) shows no
  significant signal at any K.
- **MI complications**: of 7 BH survivors, only 3 are also bootstrap-robust
  — all involving the ZSN (cardiac) complication, via MI per-feature
  (K=3, K=5) and DII+L1 (K=16).
- **Trading**: DII+L1 robust at K=10 (+0.019 [0.011, 0.026]) and K=16
  (+0.016 [0.011, 0.026]); the only other robust result is II per-feature at
  K=3. MI per-feature shows essentially no signal.

**Extended methods, evaluated on the synthetic benchmark only:**
- **RF** has the best raw ranking accuracy of any method (bootstrap τ up to
  0.50, CI never crosses zero) but **never detects the XOR synergy pair**
  well (rank 14–46, worst of all six methods) — permutation importance
  dilutes across correlated features and misses pure interaction effects.
- **MINE** does not clearly converge within the compute budget used (18.3%
  relative change at epoch 300 vs. a 5% threshold) and its bootstrap CI
  crosses zero at every p — unreliable at this training budget.
- **InfoNCE** (p=27 only, single point estimate, not bootstrapped) underperforms
  all four core methods (τ = −0.066 to 0.134) and shows no significant
  downstream advantage over a random baseline.

